    "caseText": "\n\n<p><strong>Horizon Consumer Goods Ltd. (HCGL)</strong> is a national distributor of personal healthcare and packaged foods. In preparation for its five-year strategic blueprint, the Board directed the newly appointed Chief Financial Officer, Mr. Alok Verma, to revamp the corporate financial planning architecture.</p>\n<p>Mr. Verma explained to the senior leadership team that <strong>Financial Planning</strong> is the operational process of estimating the capital requirement of an enterprise and determining its financing composition. It ensures that neither is the enterprise starved of operational liquidity (avoiding technical insolvency) nor does it accumulate unproductively idle cash balances (which depress Return on Capital Employed).</p>\n<p>The financial planning framework at HCGL is bifurcated into: (1) <strong>Long-term Financial Planning</strong> (3 to 5 years), focusing on capital expenditure appraisal, capital structure design, and long-term funding sources, and (2) <strong>Short-term Financial Planning</strong> (1 year or quarterly), focusing on operating cash budgets, working capital management, and lines of credit. Mr. Verma emphasized that the sales forecast is the critical cornerstone upon which all pro-forma statements and resource allocations are anchored.</p>\n",
