    "caseText": "\n\n<p><strong>Zenith Consumer Retail Ltd. (ZCRL)</strong> is an omnichannel retail chain operating supermarkets and hypermarkets across tier-1 and tier-2 Indian cities. The company’s Board of Directors is conducting a strategic review to ensure that its financial policies (Capital Structure, Dividend Policy, and Working Capital Policy) are tightly aligned with its corporate competitive strategy.</p>\n<p>The Group CFO, Mr. Manish Singhal, presented three foundational strategic alignment doctrines:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Risk Balancing Doctrine:</strong> High business risk (operational earnings volatility due to intense consumer price wars) must be counterbalanced by low financial risk (conservative debt levels), whereas low business risk allows higher debt capacity.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dividend Policy Signaling:</strong> Under Lintner’s dividend smoothing model, dividend changes communicate managerial insider confidence regarding the permanence of future cash flows; erratic dividend fluctuations trigger adverse market signaling.</li>\n  <li><strong>Working Capital Policy Spectrum:</strong> An 'Aggressive' working capital policy relies heavily on cheap short-term debt to fund permanent current assets (maximizing ROE but elevating refinancing default risk), whereas a 'Conservative' policy funds permanent current assets with long-term capital (safeguarding solvency at the expense of higher interest costs).</li>\n</ol>\n",
