    "caseText": "\n\n<p><strong>AeroTech Defense Systems Ltd. (ADSL)</strong> is a high-precision aerospace engineering enterprise that manufactures avionics subsystems and missile guidance navigation modules for defense armed forces. During its annual risk governance audit, the Board of Directors constituted an Enterprise Risk Management (ERM) committee headed by the Chief Risk Officer (CRO), Dr. Vikram Malhotra.</p>\n<p>Dr. Malhotra presented an overarching enterprise risk classification taxonomy categorizing risks into:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Strategic Risk:</strong> Risks arising from flawed corporate strategy, technological disruption, or misjudging defense procurement modernization cycles.</li>\n  <li><strong>Operational Risk:</strong> The risk of loss resulting from inadequate or failed internal processes, people, systems, or external events (including supply disruption, hardware defects, and cyber intrusions).</li>\n  <li><strong>Financial Risk:</strong> Exposure to unexpected adverse changes in market prices, including interest rate volatility on term borrowings, foreign exchange rate fluctuations on imported sensor components, and customer payment defaults.</li>\n  <li><strong>Compliance and Regulatory Risk:</strong> Penalties and license revocations resulting from non-compliance with Directorate General of Aeronautical Quality Assurance (DGAQA) standards, defense export control laws, and SEBI (LODR) regulations.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The CRO underscored the crucial distinction between <strong>Systematic (Market) Risk</strong> (non-diversifiable risk stemming from macroeconomic, geopolitical, and monetary shocks) and <strong>Unsystematic (Idiosyncratic) Risk</strong> (diversifiable firm-specific risk stemming from operational failures or managerial actions).</p>\n",
