    "caseText": "\n\n<p><strong>Alpha Capital Asset Management Ltd. (ACAML)</strong> manages institutional fixed-income and equity portfolios. The investment committee is codifying its market risk monitoring charter around <strong>Value at Risk (VaR)</strong>.</p>\n<p>The Head of Quantitative Risk, Ms. Nandita Roy, explained that VaR has become the universal standard metric across banks, mutual funds, and regulatory authorities for quantifying financial market risk. VaR answers a single straightforward question: <em>\"What is the maximum loss that an investment portfolio is expected to suffer over a specified holding period, at a chosen statistical confidence level, under normal market conditions?\"</em></p>\n<p>Ms. Roy outlined the three core structural pillars of VaR:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Specified Time Horizon ($T$):</strong> The holding period over which risk is measured (e.g., 1 day for daily trading desks, 10 days for Basel market risk capital, or 1 month for pension funds).</li>\n  <li><strong>Statistical Confidence Level ($1 - alpha$):</strong> The probability that actual losses will not exceed the VaR estimate (e.g., 95% or 99%).</li>\n  <li><strong>Maximum Loss Quantum:</strong> Expressed as a percentage of portfolio value or in absolute currency terms (Rupees).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>She also cautioned the committee regarding standard critical $Z$-values under the normal distribution: $Z_{0.90} = 1.282$, $Z_{0.95} = 1.645$, and $Z_{0.99} = 2.326$ (or $2.33$).</p>\n",
