    "caseText": "\n\n<p><strong>AeroTech Avionics & Defense Ltd. (AADL)</strong> evaluates high-technology capital expenditure projects ranging from stealth drone sub-assemblies to military satellite transponders. The Chief Risk Officer (CRO) and Head of Capital Allocation presented a seminar to the Board regarding the multi-dimensional taxonomy of risk in capital investment decisions.</p>\n<p>The presentation delineated three distinct levels of project risk:</p>\n<ol>\n  <li><strong>Standalone Risk:</strong> The risk of the project viewed in total isolation, ignoring both the company's other investments and shareholder portfolio diversification. It is measured by the dispersion of project returns (Standard Deviation or Coefficient of Variation).</li>\n  <li><strong>Corporate (Within-Firm) Risk:</strong> The contribution of a project to the overall volatility of the firm’s total corporate cash flows. It recognizes that an individual project may be risky on a standalone basis, but if its cash flows are counter-cyclical or uncorrelated with existing operations, it dampens total corporate earnings volatility.</li>\n  <li><strong>Market (Systematic) Risk:</strong> The risk of the project from the perspective of a well-diversified equity investor holding a broad market portfolio. Under the Capital Asset Pricing Model (CAPM), unsystematic firm risks are diversified away, leaving only systematic market beta ($\beta$) as the relevant pricing factor.</li>\n</ol>\n",
