    "caseText": "\n\n<p><strong>Deccan Housing Capital Ltd. (DHCL)</strong> pooled ₹1,000.00 Crore of prime residential housing loans into <strong>DSFCT Collateralized Mortgage Obligation (CMO) Series 2024-1</strong>. To attract yield-sensitive insurance companies and conservative pension funds, DHCL engineered a sophisticated structure comprising <strong>Planned Amortization Class (PAC) Bonds</strong> and <strong>Support / Companion Bonds</strong>.</p>\n<p>The CMO capital structure is partitioned as follows:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>PAC Class P-1:</strong> Principal = ₹650.00 Crore; Coupon = 7.75% per annum. Structured with a sinking fund schedule guaranteed to be maintained as long as the underlying mortgage pool prepayments remain within a designated <strong>Prepayment Collar of 100% to 250% PSA</strong> (Public Securities Association prepayment benchmark).</li>\n  <li><strong>Support / Companion Class S-1:</strong> Principal = ₹350.00 Crore; Coupon = 9.50% per annum. Acts as the volatility shock-absorber. Whenever prepayments exceed the PAC schedule, excess principal is redirected to pay down Class S-1 first. Conversely, if prepayments slow down, principal payments to Class S-1 are halted to ensure the PAC schedule is met.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The Chief Risk Officer (CRO) presented an analytical stress-test of two extreme interest rate and prepayment scenarios to the Investment Committee:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li><strong>Scenario A (Aggressive Monetary Easing):</strong> RBI cuts the repo rate by 150 bps. Mortgage refinancing spikes, and pool prepayment surges to <strong>350% PSA</strong> (substantially above the 250% upper collar). Prepayments completely pay off Class S-1 within 18 months, causing a \"Busted Support\" event.</li>\n  <li><strong>Scenario B (Severe Stagflation / Tightening):</strong> RBI hikes rates by 200 bps. Mortgage prepayments plummet to <strong>40% PSA</strong> (well below the 100% lower collar). Total pool principal collected is insufficient to meet the scheduled PAC sinking fund requirement.</li>\n</ul>\n",
