    "difficulty": "easy",
    "caseText": "\n\n<p><strong>Konark Debt Valuation Analytics (KDVA)</strong> provides independent fair-value pricing and risk analytics for asset-backed securities (ABS) and residential mortgage-backed securities (RMBS) traded on Indian debt platforms. During a briefing for institutional debt analysts, the Chief Analytical Officer discussed the core differences between valuing standard bullet corporate bonds versus amortizing securitized debt instruments.</p>\n<p>The presentation highlighted that standard corporate bonds repay their entire principal in a single bullet repayment at legal final maturity, whereas securitized debt instruments repay principal progressively across each payment period. Consequently, using 'Legal Final Maturity' to price or benchmark securitized debt severely distorts yield and risk measurements. Instead, the market universally prices securitized instruments on the basis of their <strong>Weighted Average Life (WAL)</strong>.</p>\n<p>To illustrate, KDVA analyzed a 4-year retail equipment loan pool of ₹100.00 Crore that amortizes through four equal annual principal installments of ₹25.00 Crore at the end of Year 1, Year 2, Year 3, and Year 4.</p>\n",
