        "question": "How is the Weighted Average Life (WAL) of an amortizing securitized debt instrument mathematically defined?",
        "options": [
          "The legal final maturity date stated in the trust indenture divided by two",
          "The weighted average time until each Rupee of principal is repaid, where the weights represent the proportion of principal repaid in each time period",
          "The total coupon interest paid over the life of the loan divided by the initial loan balance",
          "The arithmetic average of the highest and lowest interest rates observed in the market"
        ],
        "answer": 1,
        "explanation": "Weighted Average Life (WAL) measures the average number of years each Rupee of unpaid principal remains outstanding. It is calculated by multiplying the time period (t) by the principal repaid in that period, summing these products over all periods, and dividing by the total initial principal: WAL = Σ [t × Principal_t] / Total Principal."
