    "difficulty": "medium",
    "caseText": "\n\n<p><strong>Vindhyachal Wealth Advisors & Family Office (VWAFO)</strong> manages the investment portfolio of an ultra-high-net-worth family. During an asset allocation review, the client inquired about the long-term compounding impact of investing in <strong>Direct Plans</strong> versus <strong>Regular Plans</strong> of open-ended mutual fund schemes.</p>\n<p>VWAFO analyzed an initial lump-sum investment of <strong>₹50.00 Lakh</strong> in a premier diversified Large & Mid Cap Equity Fund over a <strong>15-year horizon</strong> under identical underlying market conditions:</p>\n<ul>\n  <li>Gross Portfolio Return: <strong>14.00% per annum</strong> compounding annually (generated by the master portfolio of stocks before any expense deductions).</li>\n  <li><strong>Regular Plan:</strong> Incurs an annual Total Expense Ratio (TER) of <strong>2.10% per annum</strong>, consisting of a 1.25% AMC operating and management fee plus a <strong>0.85% annual distributor trail commission</strong> paid to the financial broker.</li>\n  <li><strong>Direct Plan:</strong> Incurs an annual TER of <strong>1.25% per annum</strong>, eliminating the 0.85% distributor commission entirely.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Both plans maintain identical investment portfolios, the same fund manager, and identical daily stock transactions. VWAFO computed the future terminal value of both options to illustrate the cumulative drag of distributor commissions over time.</p>\n",
