    "caseText": "Oriental Trans-World Logistics Ltd. (OTWLL) is a Mumbai-headquartered global supply-chain and multimodal freight forwarding company with extensive international operations. During an annual strategic risk management meeting, the Chief Financial Officer (CFO) reviews the firm's global currency risk profile across four distinct operational streams:\n\n1. Exposure A (Freight Invoicing): Contractual freight receivables of USD 3,500,000 from North American shipping clients, due for settlement in 60 days.\n2. Exposure B (Foreign Subsidiary Reporting): OTWLL owns 100% equity in a Singapore subsidiary, Singapore Logistics Pte. Ltd., which holds net assets of SGD 50,000,000 that must be consolidated into OTWLL's INR financial statements at the end of each financial year under Ind AS 21.\n3. Exposure C (Long-term Market Competitiveness): Over a 3- to 5-year strategic horizon, a persistent structural appreciation of the Indian Rupee against the Euro and British Pound could impair OTWLL's price competitiveness against Malaysian and Vietnamese freight operators in European shipping routes.\n4. Exposure D (Vessel Charter Debt): Outstanding foreign currency syndicated loan of EUR 8,000,000 availed for acquiring container feeder vessels, requiring semi-annual debt service.\n\nThe board requests the risk committee to categorize each exposure into Transaction, Translation, or Economic exposure, assess the balance sheet and profit-and-loss implications under Ind AS 21, and evaluate internal hedging techniques—specifically natural matching.",
