    "caseText": "Global Infrastructure Finance Ltd. (GIFL), an Indian infrastructure finance NBFC, and Pacific Shipping Corporation (PSC), a US maritime logistics conglomerate, are structuring cross-border borrowings to finance long-term capital investments:\n\n- GIFL requires a 5-year loan of \\$50,000,000 to fund offshore container acquisitions.\n- PSC requires an equivalent 5-year loan of ₹4,150,000,000 to expand port warehousing facilities in Mumbai.\n- The current Spot Exchange Rate is USD / INR = ₹83.0000 (\\$50,000,000 = ₹4,150,000,000).\n\nThe direct 5-year fixed borrowing rates available to each firm in the domestic and international capital markets are as follows:\n- GIFL (Indian NBFC): INR Market = 8.00% p.a. | USD Market = 6.50% p.a.\n- PSC (US Multinational): INR Market = 9.20% p.a. | USD Market = 5.50% p.a.\n\nA leading international investment bank (Swap Bank) offers to structure a 5-year fixed-to-fixed Currency Swap between the two counterparties. The Swap Bank charges an intermediation fee of 0.20% p.a., with the remaining net savings to be shared equally between GIFL and PSC.\n\nThe treasury teams must analyze the Principle of Comparative Advantage, compute the total Quality Spread Differential (QSD), determine the net borrowing rates achieved by each company, and understand the exchange of principal amounts.",
