    "caseText": "Shivalik Commercial Bank Ltd. (SCBL) is a scheduled commercial bank in India. The bank's Asset-Liability Management Committee (ALCO) meets monthly to review interest rate sensitivity and structural liquidity mismatches across various time buckets. At the latest review, the Chief Risk Officer presents the 90-day time bucket repricing profile:\n\n- Rate-Sensitive Assets (RSA) maturing or repricing within 90 days: ₹4,000 Crore (comprising floating-rate commercial loans linked to Repo, short-term treasury bills, and call money placements).\n- Rate-Sensitive Liabilities (RSL) maturing or repricing within 90 days: ₹5,000 Crore (comprising bulk term deposits, certificate of deposits, and interbank borrowings).\n- The current weighted average yield on RSA is 8.50% p.a., and the current weighted average cost of RSL is 6.00% p.a.\n\nMonetary Policy Scenarios Evaluated by ALCO:\n- Scenario 1 (Monetary Tightening): The Reserve Bank of India (RBI) Monetary Policy Committee (MPC) hikes the policy repo rate by 75 basis points (+0.75%), resulting in an immediate 75 bps parallel rise in all short-term interest rates.\n- Scenario 2 (Monetary Easing): The RBI cuts policy rates by 50 basis points (-0.50%), leading to a 50 bps parallel decline in short-term interest rates.\n\nALCO is tasked with determining the 90-day Interest Rate Gap, computing the Rate Sensitivity Ratio (RSA/RSL), calculating the projected impact on Net Interest Income (\\(\\Delta \\text{NII}\\)) under both scenarios, and formulating appropriate balance sheet immunization strategies.",
