    "caseText": "Horizon Infrastructure Finance Ltd. (HIFL) is an infrastructure financing non-banking financial company (NBFC) in New Delhi. HIFL has sanctioned a ₹200 Crore credit facility to a green expressway concessionaire disbursed in two distinct phases:\n- Phase 1: ₹100 Crore disbursed immediately for 12 months.\n- Phase 2: ₹100 Crore disbursed in 6 months for a 6-month term.\n\nFunding Structure & Rolling Exposure:\n- HIFL funds its lending by borrowing floating-rate commercial paper linked to 3-month MIBOR + 150 basis points (1.50%), repricing quarterly at months 3, 6, and 9.\n- To hedge its cost of funds against volatile quarterly rate hikes, HIFL's treasury executes an 'FRA Strip' consisting of three consecutive forward rate agreements with ICICI Bank:\n  - Contract 1 (\\(3 \\times 6\\) FRA on ₹100 Crore): Agreed rate 7.20% p.a. (91 days).\n  - Contract 2 (\\(6 \\times 9\\) FRA on ₹200 Crore): Agreed rate 7.50% p.a. (92 days).\n  - Contract 3 (\\(9 \\times 12\\) FRA on ₹200 Crore): Agreed rate 7.85% p.a. (92 days).\n- Assume actual/365 day count convention.\n\nLive Market Outcomes at Fixing Dates:\n- At t = 3 months: 3-month MIBOR fixes at 8.00% p.a.\n- At t = 6 months: 3-month MIBOR fixes at 8.50% p.a., but simultaneous liquidity tightening causes commercial paper credit spreads to widen by 40 bps (HIFL's borrowing margin rises from +150 bps to +190 bps).\n\nHIFL's Chief Risk Officer directs the asset-liability desk to calculate the cash settlement amounts for the \\(3 \\times 6\\) and \\(6 \\times 9\\) FRAs, evaluate basis risk and credit spread risk, and contrast an FRA strip with interest rate futures.",
